@@ -18,6 +18,26 @@ Windows:          %APPDATA%\herdr\config.toml
 
 Run `herdr --help` to see the resolved config path for your system.
 
+A machine-local config can inherit one parent with a top-level `extends` value:
+
+```toml
+extends = "../shared/herdr.toml"
+
+[ui]
+sidebar_width = 28
+```
+
+Relative parent paths resolve from the file that declares them. `~` and `~/...`
+resolve from your home directory. Each parent may extend one more parent, up to
+16 files in the complete chain. Missing files, invalid TOML, unsupported
+`~user` paths, cycles, and deeper chains reject the complete load with a
+source-file diagnostic.
+
+Herdr merges the oldest parent first and the selected config last. TOML tables
+merge recursively. A child scalar, array, array of tables, or value of another
+type replaces the parent value. The `extends` key controls loading only; it is
+not part of the effective runtime config.
+
 Print the full default config:
 
 ```bash
@@ -48,6 +68,14 @@ herdr server reload-config
 
 You can also open the global menu in Herdr and choose `reload config`.
 
+Reload reads the complete inheritance chain again. A broken parent keeps the
+current running config, just like an invalid selected config. Settings screens,
+`config set-channel`, onboarding, and `config reset-keys` write only the
+selected child file. They never modify a parent or copy inherited values into
+the child. Removing a child override therefore reveals the inherited value on
+the next reload. `config reset-keys` cannot remove keybindings declared by a
+parent; override them in the child or edit that parent directly.
+
 Reload applies most UI settings without restarting panes. Startup-only settings still need a restart.
 
 Themes, sidebar layouts, copy behavior, and other presentation settings come from the client's local config, including when viewing an SSH machine. Pane defaults, worktrees, integrations, and custom commands belong to the server where the panes run. The UI's `reload config` action reloads both the client's local settings and the selected server's config. Local keybindings reload too; `--remote-keybindings server` instead uses the selected server's keybindings.
